    "allowAdjust": true,
    "explanation": "Different purposes call for different combinations of properties. (a) Smoke alarm: an alpha emitter with a long half-life. Alpha's short range is ideal — it ionises the air gap inside the alarm, allowing smoke detection, but cannot escape to harm the user. The half-life must be long (often hundreds of years, as with americium-241) so the alarm's activity stays roughly constant over the device's working life. (b) Paper thickness gauge: a beta emitter, ideally with a long half-life. Beta is matched to paper thickness because it is partially absorbed (alpha would not pass through paper at all; gamma would pass without measurable absorption). A long half-life means the gauge keeps working without recalibration as the source decays. (c) Medical imaging tracer: a gamma emitter with a short half-life. Gamma is needed because it escapes the body to reach the detector and ionises tissue least, minimising dose. The short half-life (often hours) means the patient is not radioactive for long after the scan. (d) Spent nuclear fuel: a mixture of many fission product isotopes, including some with very long half-lives. This mixture is not chosen — it is dictated by the physics of fission. The presence of long-lived isotopes in the mixture is exactly what makes the waste a long-term storage problem. Across all cases, the type of radiation, the half-life, and the chemistry of the isotope all matter, and matching them to the use is the engineering of radiation applications.",
