        "prompt": "Why is the choice of isotope for spent nuclear fuel described as 'not really a choice', unlike the other cases?",
        "explanation": "For smoke alarms, gauges, and medical tracers, scientists and engineers choose an isotope deliberately to fit the use, picking the right type of radiation, half-life, and chemistry. For spent nuclear fuel, by contrast, the mixture of isotopes is dictated by the physics of fission: when uranium-235 splits, the products vary, but they always include a wide range of isotopes with various half-lives. We don't choose to have long-lived isotopes in the waste; they are an unavoidable consequence of the reaction. This is why nuclear waste is a long-term storage problem rather than just a 'pick a better isotope' problem: the difficult-to-manage isotopes are an inherent part of how fission works."
